{
  "dom_id": "step-e12741",
  "name": "Example: Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers",
  "problem": "$\\qquad$ $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\,$ $\\pi x$ is irrational\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n- There are rational numbers such that $\\pi x$  is irrational.\n\n- The product of any rational number and $\\pi$ is irrational\n\n- There exists a rational number $x$ such that $\\pi x$ is rational.",
  "worked_solution": "The universal quantifier \"$\\forall$\" means \"for all.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **For all** $x \\in \\mathbb{Q}, \\,$ $\\pi x$ is irrational.\n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ The product of any rational number and $\\pi$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** This statement is false since zero is rational and $\\pi\\cdot 0$ is also rational.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
